## From neurons to layers

The basic building block of a neural network is the **neuron**. A neuron takes several numbers as inputs and turns them into one output number. It does this in two steps.

First it computes a **weighted sum** of its inputs. Each input is multiplied by its **weight**, which says how much that input matters. The products are added, and then a **bias** is added to the total. Second, the result goes through a non-linear **activation function**.

::image{id="img_1"}

::widget{ref="w1"}

### Many neurons make a layer

A **layer** stacks many neurons that read the same input vector. Each neuron has its own weights, so one layer produces one output value per neuron.

The activation is what makes a stack of layers more expressive than a single linear transformation. Without it, the layers would add no more power than one linear step.

::image{id="img_2"}

In a **fully connected layer** every neuron reads every output of the previous layer. The number of weights therefore grows as the product of the two layer sizes, which is why wider layers cost more to train.

::quiz{ref="q1"}

## Activation functions

The activation function decides how strong the neuron's output is. Two examples:

- A **rectified linear unit (ReLU)** passes positive values unchanged and turns negative values into zero.
- The **sigmoid** squeezes the output into a bounded range.

::widget{ref="w2"}

::quiz{ref="q2"}

